From the problem, we can start filling up the initial values and read the diagonal:
f(1,1) = 1;
f(1,2) = 2;
f(1,3) = 2;
f(1,4) = 2;
f(2,2) = 3;
f(2,3) = 5;
f(2,4) = 7;
f(2,5) = 10;
f(3,3) = 6;
(...
We can see that these values indeed match the binomial coefficients in Pascal's triangle. Hence, 
f(m,n) = $\binom{m+n-2}{n-1}.$
</think>
Now, we need to find
$$S(k) = \sum_{a+b=k} f(a,b)=\sum_{a+b=k}\binom{a+b-2}{b-1}=\binom{k-1}{k-1}+\binom{k-1}{k-2}+\binom{k-1}{k-3}+\cdots+\binom{k-1}{0},$$
since $a+b=k$. We recognize this as the sum of all binomial coefficients from $\binom{k-1}{0}$ to $\binom{k-1}{k-1}$, which is equivalent to $2^{k-1}$, by the binomial theorem.
 thus,
$$S(k)=2^{k-1}.$$

Applying the above identity into the given summation that turns to:
$$S(k+2) = \sum_{a+b=k+2} f(a,b) = 2^{k+1}.$$
So, we now have
$$2^{k+1} = p\cdot 2^{k} + q\cdot 2^{k-1}.$$
Dividing by $2^{k-1}$, we get
$$4 = 2p + q,$$
so
$$p+q=4,\text{ and }\quad p=2, \exists q=2.$$
Hence, the answer is $\boxed{4}.$

